<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1372–<a href="/years/1449">1449</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani was an Egyptian scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> jurist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, where he served several terms as chief judge. His Fath al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, a commentary on the hadith collection of al-Bukhari, became the standard work of its kind, and he also wrote biographical dictionaries and histories. He is regarded as one of the greatest hadith scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-hajar-al-asqalani.jpg" alt="Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fouad GM · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_al-hafidh_Ibn_Hajar_Al-Asqalani_(1)._Cairo,_Egypt_%C2%A9_Fouad_Gehad_Marei_(2009).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
